{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdUaUQDt2MCyUJq8oPm1Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014VA9ty7b8STcr2CAdGEjBS","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver/docs/aws-edge-integration.md","old_string":"- Traefik still owns `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host for anything reaching it directly;\n  it does not conflict with DMS because DMS never publishes those ports to the host — it binds\n  them only inside the `mail-edge-wg` network namespace, reachable exclusively via the tunnel.","new_string":"- Traefik still binds `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host (no port conflict with DMS, which\n  never publishes those ports to the host — it binds them only inside the `mail-edge-wg`\n  netns). But **Traefik's TCP routers for mail were discovered from Docker labels on the old\n  `mailserver` container, which no longer has any** (`network_mode: service:*` forbids\n  labels). Confirmed broken: `mail.ai-servicers.com:993` now TLS-handshakes against Traefik's\n  cert but returns `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request` instead of proxying IMAP — Traefik has no\n  matching router and is falling through to its HTTP stack. **This means any client that\n  reached DMS via Traefik before this refactor (e.g. Nextcloud Mail at\n  `mail.ai-servicers.com:993`/`:587`) is currently broken**, not just theoretically at risk.\n  Fixing it is a Traefik-project change (new label source or a file-provider route pointing at\n  `mail-edge-wg`) — out of scope for this brief; see `suggested_follow_ups`."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":558,"cache_read_input_tokens":113713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":558},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4887fea5-78a1-4b14-a071-ae362ca71a13","uuid":"aae11e53-555d-4793-bee1-bc85f7d1cbf8","timestamp":"2026-07-28T14:01:38.440Z","request_id":"req_011CdUaUMtz2xpXvvhGQxoeT"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014VA9ty7b8STcr2CAdGEjBS","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver/docs/aws-edge-integration.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4887fea5-78a1-4b14-a071-ae362ca71a13","uuid":"cf3f95ca-10c3-4a6d-863b-c817b730e8e1","timestamp":"2026-07-28T14:01:38.548Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver/docs/aws-edge-integration.md","oldString":"- Traefik still owns `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host for anything reaching it directly;\n  it does not conflict with DMS because DMS never publishes those ports to the host — it binds\n  them only inside the `mail-edge-wg` network namespace, reachable exclusively via the tunnel.","newString":"- Traefik still binds `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host (no port conflict with DMS, which\n  never publishes those ports to the host — it binds them only inside the `mail-edge-wg`\n  netns). But **Traefik's TCP routers for mail were discovered from Docker labels on the old\n  `mailserver` container, which no longer has any** (`network_mode: service:*` forbids\n  labels). Confirmed broken: `mail.ai-servicers.com:993` now TLS-handshakes against Traefik's\n  cert but returns `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request` instead of proxying IMAP — Traefik has no\n  matching router and is falling through to its HTTP stack. **This means any client that\n  reached DMS via Traefik before this refactor (e.g. Nextcloud Mail at\n  `mail.ai-servicers.com:993`/`:587`) is currently broken**, not just theoretically at risk.\n  Fixing it is a Traefik-project change (new label source or a file-provider route pointing at\n  `mail-edge-wg`) — out of scope for this brief; see `suggested_follow_ups`.","originalFile":"# Running behind the AWS mail-edge\n\nHow this `docker-mailserver` (DMS) stack works when its public ports are terminated on an\nAWS edge and tunnelled home, instead of being exposed directly. The **AWS half** lives in\n[`~/projects/aws/mail-edge/README.md`](/home/administrator/projects/aws/mail-edge/README.md);\nread both — they are one system.\n\n## Why this is needed\n\nHome is on **Verizon residential**: inbound 25 is blocked, the IP is dynamic, and it's on the\nSpamhaus PBL so outbound mail is rejected. The AWS edge gives us a public Elastic IP with\nreverse DNS. DMS stays the **single source of truth** for all mail config; AWS is a dumb pipe.\n\n## The one thing that must change here: PROXY protocol\n\nIf the edge just TCP-forwards, DMS would see **every** connection as coming from the edge's\ntunnel IP — breaking Rspamd scoring, greylisting, and Fail2ban (they'd all act on one IP).\nThe fix is **PROXY protocol**: HAProxy on the edge prepends the real client IP, and DMS is\nconfigured to trust and read it. Postfix and Dovecot both support it. [DMS tutorial][dmsproxy]\n\n> ✅ **Applied and verified 2026-07-28** against DMS v15.x. The upstream tutorial's exact\n> directives needed adjusting for this deployment (below) — there's still an open request to\n> make this env-var-driven ([#3866][iss]) that may simplify it in a future DMS release.\n\n### Postfix — `config/postfix-master.cf` only (deliberately not `postfix-main.cf`)\n\n- `smtp/inet/postscreen_upstream_proxy_protocol=haproxy` (port 25, fronted by `postscreen`)\n- `submission/inet/smtpd_upstream_proxy_protocol=haproxy` (port 587)\n- `submissions/inet/smtpd_upstream_proxy_protocol=haproxy` (port 465 — DMS calls this listener\n  `submissions`, not `smtps`)\n\nDeliberately **not** set globally in `postfix-main.cf`: that would also apply to the internal\n127.0.0.1 amavis reinjection listener, which will never receive a PROXY header and would\nbreak local delivery. Scoping per-listener in `postfix-master.cf` avoids that.\n\n### Dovecot — `config/dovecot.cf`\n\n- `haproxy_trusted_networks = 10.77.0.1/32` — trust ONLY the edge's tunnel IP; a wider CIDR\n  would let anything else reaching 993 spoof its source via a forged PROXY header.\n- `haproxy_timeout = 3 secs`\n- `service imap-login { inet_listener imaps { haproxy = yes } }` — only the `imaps` (993)\n  listener is proxied. Plain `imap` (143) doesn't need it: it's bound inside the\n  `mail-edge-wg` network namespace, unreachable from the LAN or internet.\n\n### The contract with the edge (as built)\n\nThere is **no port remapping**. `mailserver` shares `mail-edge-wg`'s network namespace\n(`network_mode: service:mail-edge-wg` in `docker-compose.yml`) instead of publishing host\nports, so Postfix/Dovecot bind `25/465/587/993` directly inside that netns — with no\nconflict against Traefik, which still owns `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host for\nanything not going through the tunnel.\n\n| Public port (edge) | → home listener | PROXY variant |\n|---|---|---|\n| 25 (postscreen) | `25` | `send-proxy-v2` |\n| 587 submission | `587` | `send-proxy-v2` |\n| 465 smtps | `465` | `send-proxy-v2` |\n| 993 imaps | `993` | `send-proxy-v2` |\n\nHAProxy on the edge sends `send-proxy-v2` uniformly to all four. Port 143 (plain IMAP) is\n**not** proxied or exposed — it stays bound inside the netns, unreachable from the LAN or\ninternet, so it needs no PROXY listener. Keep this table in lockstep with the `aws/mail-edge`\nHAProxy config — it's the seam.\n\n## Network / tunnel\n\n- WireGuard runs as its **own container**, `mail-edge-wg` (`lscr.io/linuxserver/wireguard`),\n  not as a host-level `wg-quick@wg0` unit — this host has no passwordless sudo, so an\n  autonomous worker cannot install a systemd unit. The container owns the network namespace\n  that `mailserver` joins via `network_mode: service:mail-edge-wg`; wg0/10.77.0.2 exists only\n  inside that netns. Consequence: restarting `mail-edge-wg` destroys the namespace and\n  `mailserver` must be restarted after it — Docker will not cascade that restart on its own.\n  A `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` plus a healthcheck that checks for a live `wg0`\n  (not just an open port) exists in `docker-compose.yml` to catch that failure mode.\n- Home dials **out** to the edge (outbound UDP 51820, not ISP-blocked); the edge can never\n  initiate, since home sits behind NAT on a dynamic residential IP. `PersistentKeepalive = 25`\n  keeps the NAT mapping open. Peer config lives at\n  `~/projects/secrets/mail-edge-wg.conf` (mode 600), generated by `aws/mail-edge`.\n- `haproxy_trusted_networks` (Dovecot, `config/dovecot.cf`) is scoped to `10.77.0.1/32` — the\n  edge's tunnel IP only. Postfix's proxy directives (`config/postfix-master.cf`) are scoped\n  per-listener rather than global, so the internal amavis reinjection listener on 127.0.0.1\n  never gets stuck demanding a PROXY header it will never receive.\n\n## DNS (public `ai-servicers.com`) — target state, not yet cut over\n\n| Record | Value | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| MX | `mail.ai-servicers.com` pri 10 | **not yet set** — MX still points at Cloudflare Email Routing; cutover is a separate human decision |\n| `mail` A | Elastic IP (34.194.247.228) | already set, Cloudflare proxy **off** (grey cloud) |\n| PTR | `mail.ai-servicers.com` | **AWS-side**, via the port-25 form — **still PENDING** at AWS as of 2026-07-28 |\n| SPF | `v=spf1 mx -all` (or `ip4:<EIP> -all`) | authorise the edge IP as sender — apply at cutover |\n| DKIM | generated by DMS (`setup config dkim`) | publish the TXT it emits |\n| DMARC | `v=DMARC1; p=quarantine; rua=mailto:postmaster@ai-servicers.com` | start at `p=none` to monitor |\n\nDo not send test mail to third-party domains (Gmail, Outlook, etc.) until the PTR resolves —\nthe Elastic IP has no reputation yet and premature sending risks burning it before go-live.\n\n## Accounts\n\nMailboxes/domains via **postfixadmin** (already in the compose) or `setup email add`. Nothing\nabout account management changes under the edge.\n\n## Test plan\n\n1. ✅ **Tunnel up:** `wg show` both ends; ping tunnel IPs — verified bidirectional, survives\n   container restart.\n2. ⬜ **Receive real external mail:** blocked on the MX cutover (out of scope here).\n3. ✅ **Real IP preserved:** confirmed via direct connections to the public edge — Postfix\n   (`postscreen`, `submission`, `submissions`) and Dovecot (`imap-login`) logs all show the\n   real client IP (`108.35.80.85`), not the tunnel peer (`10.77.0.1`). See log excerpts above.\n4. ⬜ **Send to a third party** (`swaks --to you@gmail.com …`): deliberately **not run** — the\n   Elastic IP's PTR is still pending, so outbound test mail to real providers risks reputation\n   damage before go-live. Test with local/self-directed delivery only until PTR resolves.\n5. ⬜ **Deliverability** (mail-tester.com, PTR via `dig -x`): blocked on PTR + MX cutover.\n6. ⬜ **Fail2ban bans on real IP, not tunnel IP:** not yet exercised — plausible given IP\n   attribution is already correct in the logs above, but not directly tested.\n\n## Current stack state (2026-07-28)\n\n- **Live and verified.** `mail-edge-wg` and `mailserver` are both up and healthy; the tunnel\n  passed a bidirectional ping test and both containers auto-start on host reboot\n  (`restart: unless-stopped`). **Recovery from a `mail-edge-wg` restart is manual, not\n  automatic**: `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` only orders container start —\n  restarting `mail-edge-wg` alone destroys the shared network namespace, and `mailserver`\n  keeps running inside the now-orphaned netns rather than being restarted by Docker. The\n  `mailserver` healthcheck detects this (it checks for a live `wg0`, not just an open port),\n  but detection ≠ recovery — restart `mailserver` by hand afterward.\n- PROXY protocol is configured and confirmed working on all four edge-forwarded ports. Direct\n  evidence from `docker logs mailserver`, same day:\n  - `postfix/postscreen: CONNECT from [108.35.80.85]:54332 to [10.60.1.79]:25` (port 25)\n  - `postfix/submission/smtpd: connect from …fios.verizon.net[108.35.80.85]` (port 587)\n  - `postfix/submissions/smtpd: connect from …fios.verizon.net[108.35.80.85]` (port 465)\n  - `dovecot: imap-login: … rip=108.35.80.85, lip=10.60.1.79` (port 993)\n\n  In every case the logged address is the real Verizon client IP, not the tunnel peer\n  `10.77.0.1` — proof the PROXY header is being read, not just accepted.\n- Traefik still owns `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host for anything reaching it directly;\n  it does not conflict with DMS because DMS never publishes those ports to the host — it binds\n  them only inside the `mail-edge-wg` network namespace, reachable exclusively via the tunnel.\n- **MX for `ai-servicers.com` still points at Cloudflare Email Routing, not this edge.** No\n  production mail flows through this path yet — cutting over is a separate, later, human\n  decision (DNS changes are explicitly out of scope for the work that built this).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Docker Mailserver — Mail Server behind a Proxy][dmsproxy]\n- [DMS issue #3866 — PROXY protocol via env (future simplification)][iss]\n\n[dmsproxy]: https://docker-mailserver.github.io/docker-mailserver/edge/examples/tutorials/mailserver-behind-proxy/\n[iss]: https://github.com/docker-mailserver/docker-mailserver/issues/3866\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":134,"oldLines":9,"newStart":134,"newLines":17,"lines":[" ","   In every case the logged address is the real Verizon client IP, not the tunnel peer","   `10.77.0.1` — proof the PROXY header is being read, not just accepted.","-- Traefik still owns `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host for anything reaching it directly;","-  it does not conflict with DMS because DMS never publishes those ports to the host — it binds","-  them only inside the `mail-edge-wg` network namespace, reachable exclusively via the tunnel.","+- Traefik still binds `0.0.0.0:{25,465,587,993}` on the host (no port conflict with DMS, which","+  never publishes those ports to the host — it binds them only inside the `mail-edge-wg`","+  netns). But **Traefik's TCP routers for mail were discovered from Docker labels on the old","+  `mailserver` container, which no longer has any** (`network_mode: service:*` forbids","+  labels). Confirmed broken: `mail.ai-servicers.com:993` now TLS-handshakes against Traefik's","+  cert but returns `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request` instead of proxying IMAP — Traefik has no","+  matching router and is falling through to its HTTP stack. **This means any client that","+  reached DMS via Traefik before this refactor (e.g. Nextcloud Mail at","+  `mail.ai-servicers.com:993`/`:587`) is currently broken**, not just theoretically at risk.","+  Fixing it is a Traefik-project change (new label source or a file-provider route pointing at","+  `mail-edge-wg`) — out of scope for this brief; see `suggested_follow_ups`."," - **MX for `ai-servicers.com` still points at Cloudflare Email Routing, not this edge.** No","   production mail flows through this path yet — cutting over is a separate, later, human","   decision (DNS changes are explicitly out of scope for the work that built this)."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
